  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1304.4737v1/blocks/prose-sec-front-matter",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Front matter",
      "pageStart": 1,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/sec-front-matter.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Toroidal domain walls as Hopﬁons\nMichikazu Kobayashi1, 2 and Muneto Nitta3\n1 Department of Basic Science, University of Tokyo,\nKomaba 3-8-1, Meguro-ku, Tokyo 153-8902, Japan\n2 Department of Physics, Kyoto University, Oiwake-cho,\nKitashirakawa, Sakyo-ku, Kyoto 606-8502, Japan\n3Department of Physics, and Research and Education Center for Natural Sciences,\nKeio University, Hiyoshi 4-1-1, Yokohama, Kanagawa 223-8521, Japan\n(Dated: April 5, 2022)\nAbstract\nWe construct stable domain walls in a shape of a torus in the Faddeev-Skyrme model with\na quadratic potential term admitting two discrete vacua.",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1304.4737v1/blocks/prose-sec-000",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "I Introduction",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/sec-000-i-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Topological solitons are ubiquitous in various subjects in physics from condensed matter\nphysics [1] to high energy physics [2] and cosmology [3]. One of the most simplest and most\nfrequently appearing solitons are kinks in d = 1 + 1 dimensions or domain walls d = 2 + 1\nor larger dimensions, which are present in various condensed matter systems such as optical\nﬁbers, ferromagnets, charge density waves, Bose-Einstein condensates, helium superﬂuids\nand so on. Domain walls are also studied in high energy physics and cosmology. In super-\nsymmetric theories, domain walls are Bogomol’nyi-Prasad-Somm",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1304.4737v1/blocks/prose-sec-001",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "II The model and solitons in 1+1 and 2+1 dimensions",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-ii-the-model-and-solitons-in-11-and-21-dimension.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Let n(x) = (n1(x), n2(x), n3(x)) be a unit three vector of scalar ﬁelds with a constraint\nn · n = 1. The Lagrangian of our model is given by (µ = 0, 1, 2, 3)\nL = 1\n2∂µn · ∂µn −L4(n) −V (n).\n(1)\nHere, the four-derivative (Faddeev-Skyrme) term is given by\nL4(n) = κF 2\nµν = κ [n · (∂µn × ∂νn)]2 = κ(∂µn × ∂νn)2\n(2)\nwith a “ﬁeld strength,”\nFµν = n · (∂µn × ∂νn).\n(3)\nWe take the potential term [16, 22]\nV (n) = m2(1 −n2\n3),\n(4)\nadmitting two discrete vacua n3 = ±1 in order to allow a domain wall solution. In condensed\nmatter physics, this potential is well known in ferromagnets, so we call it ferroma",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
