      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "III Toroidal domain walls in 3+1 dimensions",
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-iii-toroidal-domain-walls-in-31-dimensions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The domain wall ring can be linearly extended to a domain wall tube in d = 3 + 1\ndimensions. Here we further consider a closed domain wall tube, i.e., a toroidal domain\nwall. Such a toroidal domain wall is unstable unless the U(1) modulus is twisted along the\ntorus. Therefore, we twist the U(1) modulus along the torus, resulting in a doubly twisted\ntoroidal domain wall. First, let us put a charge Q (or −Q) lump in the y > 0 (or y < 0)\nregion of the y-z plane,\nQ = 1\n4π\nZ\nx=0,y>0\ndydz F23 = −1\n4π\nZ\nx=0,y<0\ndydz F23.\n(8)\nThen, let us rotate it around the z-axis with twisting the U(1) modulus P ti",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
